&lt;p data-pm-slice=&quot;1 1 []&quot;&gt;Iran has begun distributing iodine tablets to people living near its nuclear facilities amid concerns that the United States could launch fresh military strikes against the country, according to a report by The Times of London, which cited an Iranian Health Ministry official.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&quot;Yes, we are distributing iodine,&quot; the official was quoted as saying. &quot;Trump can no longer be trusted. He may go through with this crime,&quot; Al Jazeera reported.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The official said Iranian authorities had routinely distributed the preventive medication to residents living within a two-to-three-kilometre radius of nuclear installations during periods of heightened military conflict.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The reported move comes as US President Donald Trump weighs whether to resume military operations against Iran, with American officials confirming that his national security team has held discussions on the issue.&lt;/p&gt;
